WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.967
Two months had passed since Mr. Bumble had married Mrs. Corney, the matron of the workhouse.

2
00:00:05.968 --> 00:00:10.055
Mr. Bumble had become the master of the workhouse, but he was unhappy.

3
00:00:10.056 --> 00:00:18.221
He no longer wore the gold-lace trimmed coat and the cocked hat of the parish officer. And he quarreled constantly with the new Mrs. Bumble.

4
00:00:18.222 --> 00:00:27.610
"I was misled by six teaspoons, a pair of sugar tongs, a small quantity of secondhand furniture, and twenty pounds in cash," Mr. Bumble said to himself.

5
00:00:27.611 --> 00:00:31.531
"We've been married only a short time, but it seems like forever."

6
00:00:31.532 --> 00:00:38.173
He had just had another argument with his wife. Snatching up his hat, he left the workhouse in a hurry.

7
00:00:38.174 --> 00:00:43.848
He roamed the streets until it began to rain heavily. Then he stepped inside a pub.

8
00:00:43.849 --> 00:00:48.935
Mr. Bumble went into the parlor to enjoy his drink and read the newspaper.

9
00:00:48.936 --> 00:00:58.229
There was only one other person sitting at the table: a tall man, wearing a dark cloak. The man's clothes were dusty, as if he'd traveled a great distance.

10
00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:06.650
As Mr. Bumble read the newspaper, he couldn't resist stealing a look at the stranger. And every time he did, the stranger was looking at him!

11
00:01:06.651 --> 00:01:13.221
Finally the man said, "I think I've seen you before. You used to be the parish officer, didn't you?"

12
00:01:13.222 --> 00:01:16.419
"Yes," said Mr. Bumble in surprise.

13
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.324
The stranger stood up and closed the door so no one would overhear him.

14
00:01:20.325 --> 00:01:28.808
"I came to this place today to find you. I want some information, and I'm willing to pay for it." He pushed some coins across the table.

15
00:01:28.809 --> 00:01:36.387
The master of the workhouse carefully examined the coins to be sure that they were genuine. Then he put them in his pocket.

16
00:01:36.388 --> 00:01:44.030
"Cast your mind back to the workhouse about ten or twelve years ago," said the stranger. "A boy was born there one night."

17
00:01:44.031 --> 00:01:48.863
"Many boys were. How can you expect me to remember just one?"

18
00:01:48.864 --> 00:01:56.205
"I'm talking about a meek-looking, pale boy, who was apprenticed to an undertaker. He later ran away to London."

19
00:01:56.206 --> 00:02:05.355
"Why, you mean Oliver Twist!" said Mr. Bumble. "I remember him, of course. There wasn't a worse rascal in the work—"

20
00:02:05.356 --> 00:02:10.942
"I don't want to hear about him," interrupted the stranger. "I've heard enough of him.

21
00:02:10.943 --> 00:02:16.172
I want to know about the old woman who took care of his mother. Where is she?"

22
00:02:16.173 --> 00:02:22.180
"You're talking about old Sally," said Mr. Bumble. "She died last winter."

23
00:02:22.181 --> 00:02:29.847
The stranger sat lost in thought for a few minutes. Mr. Bumble couldn't tell if he was relieved or disappointed.

24
00:02:29.848 --> 00:02:35.839
"It doesn't matter," the stranger said at last. With that, he rose as if to depart.

25
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.490
But Mr. Bumble saw an opportunity to make some more money.

26
00:02:39.491 --> 00:02:43.902
He remembered how agitated his wife had been on the night that Sally died.

27
00:02:43.903 --> 00:02:48.910
Although he didn't know everything Sally had told Mrs. Bumble, he knew it concerned Oliver.

28
00:02:48.911 --> 00:02:52.743
"There's one woman who might be able to help you," he said.

29
00:02:52.744 --> 00:02:57.489
The stranger's face seemed to fill with fear. "How can I find her?"

30
00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:59.561
"You'll have to go through me."

31
00:02:59.562 --> 00:03:07.116
The stranger wrote out an address on a scrap of paper and handed it to Mr. Bumble. "Bring her to me tomorrow night at nine o'clock."

32
00:03:07.117 --> 00:03:11.403
After that the two men left the pub, heading in opposite directions.

33
00:03:11.404 --> 00:03:17.886
But Mr. Bumble soon realized he needed one more piece of information and hurried after the stranger.

34
00:03:17.887 --> 00:03:21.473
"What do you want?" cried the man, whirling around.

35
00:03:21.474 --> 00:03:24.378
"What name am I to ask for tomorrow night?"

36
00:03:24.379 --> 00:03:28.529
"Monks!" The man strode hastily away.

37
00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:33.458
The sky was dull and overcast when Mr. and Mrs. Bumble set out the next night.

38
00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:39.696
The clouds, which had been threatening all day, were spread out densely, as if preparing for a violent thunderstorm.

39
00:03:39.697 --> 00:03:46.116
The Bumbles were both wrapped in shabby outer garments that would protect them from the rain and keep them from being recognized.

40
00:03:46.117 --> 00:03:49.942
They headed toward some run-down houses bordering the river.

41
00:03:49.943 --> 00:03:57.434
The people who lived there survived by theft and other crimes. Among these houses stood an old factory building.

42
00:03:57.435 --> 00:04:03.180
"This should be the place," said Mr. Bumble, consulting the scrap of paper in his hand.

43
00:04:03.181 --> 00:04:07.998
"Hello!" cried a voice from above. "I'll be down in a minute."

44
00:04:07.999 --> 00:04:11.156
"Is that the man?" Mrs. Bumble asked.

45
00:04:11.157 --> 00:04:13.005
Her husband nodded.

46
00:04:13.006 --> 00:04:17.172
"Then say as little as you can until we find out what he wants."

47
00:04:17.173 --> 00:04:23.830
Monks opened the door, motioned for them to come in quickly, and then shut the door. "Is this the woman?"

48
00:04:23.831 --> 00:04:27.573
Mr. Bumble only nodded, remembering his wife's warning.

49
00:04:27.574 --> 00:04:30.252
Suddenly there was a crack of thunder.

50
00:04:30.252 --> 00:04:37.219
Monks turned away for a few minutes. When he looked at them again, his face was distorted and discolored.

51
00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:42.981
"These fits come over me now and then," he said. "Thunder sometimes brings them on."

52
00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:52.878
A lantern cast a dim light. Monks motioned for them to sit down. Mr. Bumble looked nervously around, but Mrs. Bumble was calm.

53
00:04:52.879 --> 00:04:59.386
"Let's get down to business," Monks said to Mrs. Bumble. "Were you with Oliver's mother the night she died?"

54
00:04:59.387 --> 00:05:00.965
"Yes."

55
00:05:00.966 --> 00:05:05.203
"Something was taken from her. Something that she wore . . ."

56
00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:09.465
"What's the information worth to you?" Mrs. Bumble interrupted.

57
00:05:09.466 --> 00:05:12.806
"It could be twenty pounds; it could be nothing."

58
00:05:12.807 --> 00:05:17.441
"I know I have what you want. Make it twenty-five pounds."

59
00:05:17.442 --> 00:05:22.957
"Twenty-five?" Monks pulled away. "What if your information is useless?"

60
00:05:22.958 --> 00:05:28.362
"You can easily take the money from me. I'm alone and unprotected here."

61
00:05:28.363 --> 00:05:34.179
"Not alone, my dear, nor unprotected," Mr. Bumble said in a voice trembling with fear.

62
00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:42.084
Monks removed a canvas bag from his pocket and laid twenty-five pounds on the table. Mrs. Bumble began her story.

63
00:05:42.085 --> 00:05:54.179
"Sally told me she stole a gold necklace from Oliver's mother. She sold it to a pawn shop, but she told me where to find the pawn ticket. I redeemed the necklace, and . . . here it is."

64
00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:03.409
Mrs. Bumble held out a gold locket. Monks pried it open with trembling hands. Inside were two locks of hair and a wedding ring.

65
00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:13.084
The locket was inscribed Agnes, with space left for a last name. There was also a date, which was about a year before Oliver was born.

66
00:06:13.085 --> 00:06:16.988
"Is this what you wanted from me?" asked Mrs. Bumble.

67
00:06:16.989 --> 00:06:23.061
"It is," said Monks. "And now I must destroy this evidence of Oliver's past."

68
00:06:23.062 --> 00:06:27.806
He suddenly wheeled the table aside and pulled up a trapdoor in the floor.

69
00:06:27.807 --> 00:06:31.721
The three of them looked down at the river swirling angrily below.

70
00:06:31.722 --> 00:06:37.206
Monks took a deep breath and then threw the locket into the river.

